opd: one teacher per sample source, so a cross-domain run distils the way a
single-domain one does.

--opd-type megatron loads ONE teacher (--opd-teacher-load) and forwards it over
the whole batch. A cross-domain run has two domains with two separate teachers,
which left only --opd-type sglang: serve both teachers over HTTP and have the
rollout fetch their log-probs. That arrangement differs from the single-domain
arms in two ways that both change the measured quantity, not just the cost.

  1. A served teacher can fail to answer. It did: the first opd_both run lost
     samples to ServerDisconnectedError on pooled connections. A resident
     teacher cannot fail to answer -- the forward runs or the process dies.

  2. The OPD KL is student_logp - teacher_logp. With a served teacher the
     student side comes from the rollout engine's incremental decode and the
     teacher side from a separate server's one-shot prefill. Those are two
     numerical paths for the same tokens, and any systematic difference between
     them lands directly in the advantage, uniformly signed. With a resident
     teacher both sides come from the same Megatron forward (that is what
     slime-opd-decouple-student-logprobs arranges) and the difference cancels.

So: allow a teacher per sample source.

  --opd-teacher-loads retool=/ckpt/math,search-r1=/ckpt/search
  --opd-teacher-ckpt-steps retool=499,search-r1=499

Either flag also takes JSON. The pair form exists because the JSON form has to
survive Ray's entrypoint, which joins the whole command into one string: a run
reached argparse with the outer single quotes still attached, and json.loads
rejects that at character 0 with a message that mentions neither quoting nor
the flag's real problem. The pair form has no quotes, braces or spaces in it,
so there is nothing for a join to get wrong. Both spellings strip surrounding
quotes before parsing, so the wrapped case is read rather than rejected.

Each entry is loaded under its own weight tag, "teacher::<source>". The backuper
holds weight sets in pinned host memory, so N teachers cost N x model-size of
host RAM and nothing on the GPU; switching between them is a host-to-device copy
of the model, ~0.3s for a 4B, against a step measured in tens of seconds.

Each teacher is forwarded over its own source's samples alone (_forward_teachers,
since 2026-11-07): the rank's samples of that source, in the batch's order,
packed into micro-batches the way build_dp_schedule packs a step (first fit
under --max-tokens-per-gpu), through the same compute_log_prob; the outputs are
scattered back to the batch's index space, None at the other sources'
positions, and the per-sample choice by Sample.metadata['source_name'] is then
the per-index choice it always was, refusing a None. Until then every teacher
forwarded the whole batch and the other sources' rows were discarded -- with
three teachers, two thirds of the teacher forwards (77 s of the replay arms'
179 s step) -- the deliberate trade for output lists that could not misalign.
The misalignment a per-source schedule could hide (a sample trained against
another domain's teacher, invisible in the loss) is what the None guard and
tests/teacher_forward_patch_test.py are for.

A teacher whose source is absent from a rank's partition is skipped, so a
single-source run pays nothing. --opd-teacher-load is untouched and still takes
the "teacher" tag, so existing runs and their checkpoints are unaffected.

A source with no teacher raises rather than defaulting to one: silently
distilling a domain against the wrong teacher is the failure this exists to
prevent.

